[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 746,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 576,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 116346,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 267,
        "headingId": 576,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ranula is a pseudocyst that develops from the extravasation of mucin, typically located in the floor of the mouth, adjacent to the sublingual gland.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-02T13:30:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 747,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 577,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 116347,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 267,
        "headingId": 577,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-02T13:30:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 748,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 578,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 116348,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 267,
        "headingId": 578,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>DA04.5 Mucocele of salivary gland</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-02T13:30:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 749,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 579,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 116349,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 267,
        "headingId": 579,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> mucocoele; retention cyst; mucus extravasation.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-02T13:30:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 750,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 580,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 116350,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 267,
        "headingId": 580,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Simple or oral ranula; plunging or cervical ranula {15356464; 22038368; 32109322; 32809690}</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-02T13:30:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 751,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 581,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 116351,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 267,
        "headingId": 581,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ranula typically occurs in the lateral side of the floor of the mouth, in association with an excretory duct of the sublingual gland. The plunging type of ranula passes around the posterior border of the mylohyoid muscle or through a hiatus in the muscle {15356464; 20054853; 22038368; 32809690}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-02T13:30:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 752,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 582,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 116352,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 267,
        "headingId": 582,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Simple ranula presents as a painless mass in the floor of the mouth, whereas a plunging ranula presents as a neck swelling. Ranulas are mostly unilateral and unifocal but can cross the midline {15356464; 22038368; 32109322; 32809690}.</p>\r\n<h5>Imaging</h5>\r\n<p>CT and MRI images of a plunging ranula may demonstrate extension of the lesion into the submandibular space (tail sign) {31334858}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-02T13:30:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 753,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 583,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 116353,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 267,
        "headingId": 583,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ranulas are rare and have a frequency of 0.2&nbsp;cases per 1000&nbsp;people. The lesions affect people of any age but are more common in teenagers and young adults. No sex predilection has been reported {12618979; 32809690}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-02T13:30:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 754,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 584,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 116354,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 267,
        "headingId": 584,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The most common etiology is trauma to the sublingual gland, which is a spontaneous secretor that produces a continuous flow of mucin {15356464; 20054853; 22038368; 32809690}. An association between ranulas and primary Sj&ouml;gren syndrome has been reported {28964279; 31141241; 32803681}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-02T13:30:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 755,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 585,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 116355,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 267,
        "headingId": 585,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-02T13:30:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 762,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 592,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 116356,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 267,
        "headingId": 592,
        "headingText": "<p >The lesion appears as a blue and fluctuant dome-shaped mass that resembles a male frog&rsquo;s vocal sac. It ranges from 1&nbsp;to 40&nbsp;mm in size {3343879; 32809690}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-11-21T12:54:03"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 756,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 586,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 116357,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 267,
        "headingId": 586,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ranula is a cyst-like structure consisting of mucin surrounded by fibroconnective or granulation tissue that frequently contains histiocytes and is devoid of an epithelial lining. Thrombi and blood-filled vessels can be seen in the surrounding wall or the adjacent salivary gland.</p>\r\n<h5>Differential diagnosis</h5>\r\n<p>The entities include mucoepidermoid carcinoma, dermoid cyst, thyroglossal duct cyst, branchial cleft cyst, and a vascular malformation. The presence of mucin and the absence of an epithelial lining help to differentiate ranula from other cystic and vascular lesions {15356464; 22038368; 32809690}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-02T13:30:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 757,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 587,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 116358,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 267,
        "headingId": 587,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-02T13:30:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 758,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 588,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 116359,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 267,
        "headingId": 588,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-02T13:30:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 761,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 591,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 116360,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 267,
        "headingId": 591,
        "headingText": "<p><em>Essential:</em> a cyst-like structure containing mucin surrounded by fibrous or granulation tissue, without an epithelial lining.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> imaging showing extension of the lesion into the submandibular space (tail sign) (in selected cases).</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-11-21T12:54:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 759,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 589,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 116361,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 267,
        "headingId": 589,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-02T13:30:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 760,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 590,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 116362,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 267,
        "headingId": 590,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Both simple and plunging ranulas are treated by complete surgical excision of the gland with the removal of the adjacent traumatized salivary duct. Recurrence may occur due to inadequate excision {15635557; 20594633; 21757145; 32087991}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-02T13:30:28"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]